C(146,'Ramification index acts by multiplication e',[156,157,158],
'For a local inclusion of henselian DVRs of ramification index e with compatible residue algebraic closures, the map on tame inertia is multiplication by e under the root-of-uniformizer identifications. On Qℓ(1) it is invertible; on Zℓ(1) its cokernel has sizeℓ^{vℓ(e)}.',
'proved there','E','Classical tame ramification calculation; Stacks15.116 and Illusie1994§1.1 provide the structure. The page computes θ=λ(θ′)^e, giving the character exponent e. No integral isomorphism when ℓ|e.',['Stacks0EXT:15.116.2,4','Illusie1994:1.1'],['P2-S07'],comp='tame_multiplication')
C(147,'Completion preserves finite étale field extensions',[159,160,161],
'For a henselian DVR A with fraction field K and completion Khat, finite étale K-algebras and finite étale Khat-algebras are equivalent. No excellence hypothesis is needed for this statement.',
'proof sketched','A','SGA4X2.2.1 proves exactly this, by approximation/Newton and finite normalization of a henselian DVR. The manuscript deletes an excellence-based attempt and supplies a replacement. The theorem is stronger than the deleted hypothesis.',['SGA4X:2.2.1','Stacks09E8:15.113.6'],['P2-S07'])
C(148,'Inertia intersection and index',[162,163],
'For finite separable K′/K in a fixed separable closure of a henselian DVR, I′=I∩G_K′. For finite Galois extension with ramification index e and residue extension f=f_s f_i, inertia order/index contribution is e f_i, not always e.',
'proof sketched','E','Stacks15.114.5 and15.113.6 give the exact finite-level formula, including inseparable residue degree. The residual-separability shortcut must not erase f_i.',['Stacks09EE:15.114.5','Stacks09E8:15.113.6'],['P2-S07'])
C(149,'Central quotient of inertia',[164,165,166],
'If H⊂I is normal in G_K and I/H is central in G_K/H, one has a central extension1→I/H→G_K/H→G_k→1. Centrality does not assert a splitting.',
'proof sketched','E','The group-theoretic deduction is correct. The earlier notes’ nonsplitting example is separately checked:0→n Zhat→Zhat→Z/n→0 cannot split because Zhat has no nonzero finite-order element.',['Illusie1994:1.1 valuation sequence'],['P2-S07'],'A section would send1 modn to a nonzero torsion element of torsion-free Zhat.')
C(150,'Extreme point in an affine fibre',[174],
'If f maps a convex set A affinely to B, x is extreme in f(A), and y is extreme in A∩f^{-1}(x), then y is extreme in A.',
'proved there','E','Elementary convexity, independently proved here; the exact reused EVT source was searched but not securely collated. Bibliography identifies Grothendieck1954 EVT as a possible source family only.',['GrothEVT1954:bibliographic source family only'],['P2-S04'],'If y=ta+(1−t)b, extremality of x forces f(a)=f(b)=x; fibre extremality forces a=b=y.',author='Reused EVT sheet; exact edition/author attribution unresolved')
C(151,'Supporting hyperplane warning in a weak Hilbert topology',[175,176],
'A compact convex set in a Hausdorff locally convex topology need not have a continuous supporting hyperplane at every boundary point. In H=ℓ², σ(H,G) with G=span{e0+en/n} has this failure at e0 for the closed unit ball.',
'proof sketched','E','The manuscript says a missing earlier conjecture is false. The concrete modern counterexample is ours and supports that warning; it cannot identify the absent conjecture’s exact wording. Grothendieck EVT catalogue and standard separation searches do not resolve the edition.',['GrothEVT1954:bibliographic only'],['P2-S04'],'G dense ensures Hausdorff and σ(H,G) weaker than weak topology, so the ball is compact. A supporting functional at e0 must be a multiple of e0 by equality in Cauchy–Schwarz, but e0∉G.',comp='hilbert_dense_hyperplane_example',author='Reused EVT fragment; explicit counterexample reconstructed here')
C(152,'Weak topology of a separating dual pairing',[180],
'For a bilinear pairing E×F→R/C separating E, σ(E,F) is Hausdorff and its continuous dual is the image of F. Identification of F itself requires separation also on F.',
'proof sketched','E','Standard weak-topology construction, independently rechecked by domination by finitely many evaluation seminorms. One-sided and two-sided separation are different. Source-family search of EVT did not establish exact edition.',['GrothEVT1954:bibliographic only'],['P2-S04'],author='Reused EVT sheet')
C(153,'Locally convex inductive-limit universal property',[182,193],
'The locally convex direct sum is the finest locally convex topology making all summand injections continuous; a linear map out is continuous iff its restrictions to every summand are continuous. General inductive limits require the locally convex, not arbitrary final topological, construction.',
'proof sketched','E','Standard category of locally convex spaces result. The manuscript distinguishes the needed topology; no automatic preservation of arbitrary limit properties is asserted.',['GrothEVT1954:bibliographic source family'],['P2-S04'],'Universal property checked directly via continuous seminorms on each summand.',author='Reused EVT sheet')
C(154,'Completeness of a locally convex direct sum',[181,182],
'The locally convex direct sum of Hausdorff complete locally convex spaces is complete; each coordinate summand is closed and embedded with its own topology.',
'stated without proof','E','Classical EVT theorem; source-family searches found the statement but no exact original edition collation. A direct proof is included in review.md using coordinatewise completion and a seminorm obstruction to infinite support.',['GrothEVT1954:bibliographic source family'],['P2-S04'],author='Reused EVT sheet')
C(155,'Positive-cone proper addition and vague metrizability fragments',[177,178,179],
'In a product positive cone R_+^J, inverse images under addition of compact sets are closed in a compact coordinatewise box and hence compact. The manuscript’s more general cone and vague-metrizability assertions depend on hypotheses on earlier missing pages.',
'proof sketched','F','The concrete product-cone statement is correct by Tychonoff; no precise general metrizability theorem can be recovered. The marked objection to a proposition is retained without inventing the absent proposition.',['GrothEVT1954:bibliographic only'],['P2-S04'],author='Reused EVT fragment; safe model reconstructed here',confidence='medium')
C(156,'Regular blowup chart',[183],
'If (R,m) is a regular local ring with regular parameters x1,…,xn, then R[m/x1] is a regular blowup chart; modulo x1 it is the polynomial ring κ(m)[x2/x1,…,xn/x1].',
'proof sketched','E','Standard local quadratic-transform calculation. The cited Abhyankar source family was searched but the exact Lemma1 page was not identified; attribution to Grothendieck is not justified merely by reuse.',['Abhyankar1956:source family,unverified exact lemma'],['P2-S05'],'For R=k[x,y]_(x,y), write y=xt; the chart is k[x,t] at appropriate localizations and the exceptional quotient is k[t].',author='Unattributed typed local-algebra sheet')
C(157,'Common completions and ideal contraction',[184,185],
'Let R⊂Rbar be regular local domains of the same dimension with the same residue field and mRbar=mbar. Their completions are canonically isomorphic; every idealI ofR contracts from IRbar toI, principality of IRbar descends toI, and Frac(R)∩Rbar=R.',
'proof sketched','E','The entire Lemma2 is recovered by the exact printed continuation33:173→137→135. The common-completion, ideal-contraction and principality statements are independently checked; neither ring is asserted complete. The original article/edition remains unidentified, separately from the now recovered mathematical statement. Stacks10.97.3 supplies the opened faithful-flatness input.',['Abhyankar1956:unidentified exact source lead only','Stacks00MC:Lemma10.97.3'],['P2-S05'],'The regular associated graded rings are polynomial rings in the same minimal generators over the common residue field, so R/m^q≅Rbar/mbar^q for everyq. Passing to limits gives equal completions. Faithful flatness of each local completion gives ideal contraction; minimal-generator counts give descent of principality. If z/t∈Frac(R)∩Rbar then z∈tRbar∩R=tR.',author='Unattributed typed sheet',confidence='high')
C(158,'Quadratic-transform chain along a valuation',[186],
'For a regular local ring dominated by a fixed valuation, the successive local quadratic transforms are uniquely selected by the valuation. The stronger union/termination statement needs dimension and rank/residue hypotheses absent from the cropped lemma.',
'stated without proof','F','The sheet cites earlier Lemmas9/10 and[A6]. Literature search finds Abhyankar valuation/quadratic-transform work, but not the exact Lemma11–12; no universal termination assertion is inferred.',['Abhyankar1956:unverified exact lemma'],['P2-S05'],author='Unattributed reused local-algebra sheet',confidence='low')
C(159,'Symbol homomorphism modulo compact operators',[187,188],
'For the operator algebra and function space defined in the missing preceding chapter, the printed theorem asserts a surjective symbol map with compact-operator kernel. Its exact operator norm, symbol class and base manifold are not supplied here.',
'stated without proof','F','Searched exact French theorem numbers4.5.2/4.5.4, singular-integral terminology and Gegenbauer coefficients. Pseudodifferential/Calkin symbol theory is related but no exact source or scope was identified. Do not label this as Grothendieck’s new theorem.',['OperatorSource:unverified'],['P2-S06'],author='Unattributed printed operator text')
C(160,'Regularization and nonvanishing symbol',[189,190],
'The printed theorem states regularizability iff the symbol never vanishes. A parametrix modulo compact operators gives Fredholm solvability; exact equality of original and regularized solution sets additionally needs control of the kernel/error operator.',
'proof sketched','F','Exact source/definition of regularisable remains unidentified after backwards and exact-phrase searches. The residual KJ=Kf and KL=Kf formulas are ill-typed without the missing unknown/function. The modern Fredholm interpretation is explicitly a reconstruction.',['OperatorSource:unverified'],['P2-S06'],author='Unattributed printed operator text')
C(161,'Gegenbauer parity and multiplier integrals',[190,191],
'For λ>−1/2 in the standard Gegenbauer convention, C_n^λ(−t)=(−1)^n C_n^λ(t). Half-interval and log-weight integrals are finite sums of beta values and beta derivatives. The manuscript odd formula omits the n in its displayed polynomial.',
'proof sketched','F','Parity and displayed integrals were recomputed independently. The constants w_p and referenced[18a] are unavailable; the closed gamma multipliers cannot be certified at the exact printed normalization. Exact source searches were negative.',['OperatorSource:unverified'],['P2-S06'],'Code supplies general finite-sum formula and exact independent integration for λ=1/2,3/2,n=0..5; these cases do not prove a missing normalization.',comp='gegenbauer_checks',author='Unattributed printed operator text')
C(162,'Coefficient-sheaf extension of duality',[118],
'Investigate constructible duality with a sheaf of coefficient rings A_X and relative/global versions, with local noetherian/self-injective and invertibility hypotheses yet to be specified.',
'open on the pages','C','Modern six-functor frameworks treat many ringed and adic coefficients, but the exact unrestricted coefficient-sheaf claim is absent. ILO explicitly fixes Λ=Z/n in the theorem matched here.',['ILO2014:XVII0.1,6.1.1','Stacks0G2B:63.11.1'],['P2-S02'],confidence='medium')
C(163,'Strict estimates and applications',[117],
'Seek strict improvements of injective-dimension estimates under additional geometry, and possible applications. The fragment names prospects rather than a quantified inequality.',
'open on the pages','F','Modern ILOXVII6.2.4.1 is an exact quasi-injective bound and gives equality in its normalized setting. It cannot settle an unformulated strict-bound programme; scan33:165 carries question marks and qualified language.',['ILO2014:XVII6.2.4.1'],['P2-S02'],confidence='low')
C(164,'Intrinsic scheme topos construction',[195],
'The scheme étale topos can be defined intrinsically from the étale site, with representables as a generating family; comparing topology and scheme geometry motivates the construction.',
'stated without proof','A','Published SGA4VII§1–3 provides the intrinsic construction. The philosophical assertion of greater power is not independently a mathematical theorem.',['SGA4VII:§1–3'],['P2-S03'],author='Roos typescript')
C(165,'Reduction via finite maps to smooth ambient schemes',[108],
'The proposed Künneth proof seeks finite maps into smooth schemes to replace an embedding and control dimension. Such a reduction must establish the finite map and descent of the particular sheaves, not merely invoke a smooth ambient space.',
'open on the pages','C','Later finiteness/duality removes the need for this particular conditional proof. Exact finite-map construction is not completed on these pages. Noether normalization gives useful affine reductions, but not every displayed global hypothesis automatically.',['Illusie2026:§2–4','MilneLEC:24.3'],['P2-S01'],confidence='medium')
C(166,'Global adjunction in the validity sheet',[122],
'For separated finite-type f with torsion coefficients, Rf_! has right adjoint f! in an appropriate derived category; properness identifies Rf_! with Rf_*.',
'stated without proof','A','SGA4XVIII constructed étale duality in the classical scope. Stacks63.11.1 states the modern adjoint for a separated finite-type morphism of qcqs schemes and a torsion ring. The manuscript compactifiability/lissifiability qualifiers reflect its stage of construction.',['Stacks0G2B:63.11.1','Illusie2026:§2 global duality'],['P2-S01'])
C(167,'Finite-Tor cohomological Künneth and trace model',[106,110],
'For proper finite-type schemes over an algebraically closed field and finite coefficients, the derived Künneth map is an isomorphism; constructible sheaf versions require a derived tensor product (or a flat factor). Finite projective cohomology models need finite Tor amplitude.',
'proof sketched','A','Milne22.1–22.5 explicitly gives the proper theorem, derived-Tor spectral sequence and caveat of infinitely many nonzero Tor groups over Z/ℓ². This confirms the manuscript’s concern about finite-flat models.',['MilneLEC:22.1–22.5','SGA4XVII:5.2.11 via ILOXIII4.2.1'],['P2-S01'])

def codexloc(folder,scans):
 text=(ROOT/'sources/texts/codex'/f'{folder}_transcription.tex').read_text().splitlines()
 starts=[(i+1,int(re.search(r'\\scanpage\{(\d+)\}',t).group(1))) for i,t in enumerate(text) if re.search(r'\\scanpage\{(\d+)\}',t)]
 return [{'file':f'codex/{folder}_transcription.tex','cote':34,'scan':scan,'start':start,'end':starts[j+1][0]-1 if j+1<len(starts) else len(text)} for j,(start,scan) in enumerate(starts) if scan in scans]
def K(n,title,folder,scans,quote,modern,status,cls,fate,refs,search,proof='',comp=None,confidence='high'):
 C(n,title,[],modern,status,cls,fate,refs,search,proof,comp,confidence,quote,codexloc(folder,scans))

K(168,'Congruence kernel is pro-ℓ','K34-01',[18,19],'c\'est un pro-$\\ell$-groupe',
'For a finitely generated Zℓ-module E, Aut(E) is profinite and the kernel of reduction to Aut(E/ℓE) is open pro-ℓ. The indexing of Eν uses ℓ^{ν+1}.','proved there','E','A standard congruence-filtration fact; Illusie1994§1.5 repeats it in the monodromy proof. The source module label Z/ℓ^ν under Eν conflicts with its definition and is a transcription alarm.',['Illusie1994:1.5'],['P2-S09'],'Successive kernels embed in additive Hom overFℓ, hence are ℓ-groups; the inverse limit is pro-ℓ.')
K(169,'Prime-to-ℓ image is finite','K34-01',[19],'est injectif; en particulier $Q$ est fini',
'A closed pro-prime-to-ℓ subgroup of Aut(E) injects into Aut(E/ℓE), hence is finite.','proved there','E','Used explicitly by Illusie1994§1.5. Intersection with the open pro-ℓ congruence kernel is trivial.',['Illusie1994:1.5'],['P2-S09'])
K(170,'Restriction to the ℓ-primary tame factor','K34-01',[19,20],'se factorise en une représentation',
'For a continuous ℓ-adic inertia representation, after restriction to a suitable open subgroup, the prime-to-ℓ kernel has trivial image and the representation factors through an open subgroup of Zℓ(1); equivalently after finite separable field extension.','proved there','A','Grothendieck monodromy formalism, reiterated in Illusie1994§1.5. The displayed U=Ker(G→Aut(E0)) needs inverse image under u because U lies in I.',['Illusie1994:1.5','SerreTate1968:appendix source family'],['P2-S09'])
K(171,'Invariant lattice in a compact representation','K34-01',[21],'il existe toujours un ``réseau invariant''',
'A continuous finite-dimensional Qℓ-representation of a compact group admits a stable full Zℓ-lattice.','stated without proof','E','Standard compactness argument also used in Illusie1994§1.5. The factorization map π→Aut(lattice) is not generally an isomorphism, contrary to the transcribed arrow.',['Illusie1994:1.5'],['P2-S09'],'Choose a lattice L; compactness bounds all translates inside ℓ^{-N}L. Their Zℓ-span is a finite full lattice stable under the group.')
K(172,'Unipotent characteristic polynomial','K34-01',[21],'est égal à $T^n-1$',
'An n-dimensional automorphism is unipotent iff its characteristic polynomial is(T−1)^n, not T^n−1.','false as stated','E','The identity in dimension2 already refutes the literal expression. A scan check is required before deciding whether the manuscript or transcription lost parentheses.',['Illusie1994:1.1 definition of unipotence'],['P2-S09'],comp='unipotent_characteristic_polynomial')
K(173,'Quasi-unipotence and algebraic envelope','K34-01',[22,23],'revient à dire que $G^\\circ$ est unipotent',
'For a continuous finite-dimensional representation over Qℓ, quasi-unipotence is equivalent to unipotence of the identity component of its Zariski closure.','proof sketched','E','Standard algebraic-envelope criterion, compatible with the nilpotent-monodromy description in Illusie1994§1.5. Over arbitrary topological/imperfect fields, smoothness of a reduced closure requires care; the safe modern statement uses Qℓ.',['Illusie1994:1.5'],['P2-S09'],'An open subgroup has finite index, so its Zariski closure contains G°. Conversely the preimage of G° is open because the component quotient is finite.')
K(174,'Maximal unipotent subgroup and stable invariants','K34-01',[23,24,26],'$E^{U\'}=E^U=E^{G^\\circ}$',
'In characteristic0 and for a quasi-unipotent representation, the elements acting unipotently form the preimage of G°; it is the maximal open subgroup with unipotent action. Every open subgroup of it has the same invariant vectors.','proof sketched','E','Correct characteristic-zero algebraic-group deduction. Cancelled earlier filtrations are replaced by the component argument; they are not separate live counterclaims.',['Illusie1994:1.5 nilpotent description'],['P2-S09'],'A finite-order unipotent element in characteristic0 is identity. On invariants of a smaller normal open subgroup, the finite quotient acts unipotently and therefore trivially.')
K(175,'Finite-group cohomology degree','K34-01',[25],'pour $i\\geq0$',
'For finite Γ acting on a characteristic-zero vector space V, H^i(Γ,V)=0 for i>0; H^0=V^Γ generally does not vanish.','false as stated','E','Averaging proves positive-degree vanishing. Trivial Γ and V=Q give H^0=Q. The page’s ≥0 must be checked against the ink.',['MilneAV:IV2 semisimple-algebra background'],['P2-S09'],'The invariant projector is |Γ|^{-1}∑γ, making invariants exact.')
K(176,'Uniqueness of a semidirect-product splitting','K34-01',[25],'splitte de façon unique',
'Extensions of a finite group by a characteristic-zero unipotent algebraic group split under the standard hypotheses, but splittings are generally only unique up to conjugation by the unipotent radical.','false as stated','E','The literal uniqueness fails in Ga⋊C2 with inversion action: every(a,−1) gives a distinct order2 complement. H¹=0 means conjugacy of splittings, not uniqueness.',['MilneAV:IV2 averaging background'],['P2-S09'],comp='nonunique_splittings')
K(177,'Compact lattice in a vector unipotent group','K34-01',[26,27],'nécessairement isomorphe à un $\\mathbb Z_\\ell^{d\'}$',
'A compact subgroup of Qℓ^d is a free Zℓ-module of finite rank≤d; if Zariski dense in Ga^d it spans Qℓ^d and has rankd.','proved there','E','Classical local linear algebra. Closure under Zℓ scalars follows by continuity from integer multiples; boundedness makes it a submodule of a lattice.',['Illusie1994:1.5 vector-monodromy description'],['P2-S09'])
K(178,'Universal algebraic vector extension of a profinite quotient','K34-01',[27,28],'une extension \\uline{universelle}',
'Given normal open U in π with finite-rank pro-ℓ abelianization, pushing out0→U/[U,U]^(ℓ)→…→π/U through tensorQℓ gives the vector-group extension universal for continuous representations with commutative unipotent identity component and this fixed U.','proof sketched','C','This is a recognizable relative algebraic-envelope construction. The exact tensor-category universal property and transition maps are not fully stated; later Tannakian/Weil–Deligne theory is related, not an exact text collation.',['Illusie1994:1.5 Weil–Deligne discussion'],['P2-S09'],confidence='medium')
K(179,'Inertia quasi-unipotent envelope','K34-01',[28,29],'une $\\otimes$-catégorie équivalente',
'Quasi-unipotent ℓ-adic inertia representations in residue characteristic≠ℓ have a finite-image part and a commuting nilpotent operator N with twistQℓ(1), giving an algebraic-envelope description after a suitable finite quotient.','proof sketched','A','Illusie1994§1.5 gives the unique nilpotent map N:V(1)→V and exponential formula. This validates the representation description; the literal I≅Zℓ(1) on34:28 is false for full inertia, which also has wild and other tame factors.',['Illusie1994:1.5'],['P2-S09'],'The manuscript must distinguish full I, its ℓ-primary quotient, and the image of a restricted representation.')
K(180,'Finite coinvariants force solvable inertia unipotent','K34-01',[30,32,33],'est ess.\\textsuperscript{t} unipotent',
'For an extension1→I→π→π0→1, assume every open π′ has finite coinvariants of the pro-ℓ abelianization of I∩π′. A continuous Qℓ-representation with virtually solvable image of I has unipotent identity component.','proof sketched','E','The solvable case follows by passing to connected Zariski closures and killing the torus quotient: connected conjugation on a torus is trivial. The revised general-radical proof contains a separate incorrect isogeny assertion recorded next. No exact later publication of this manuscript proposition was identified.',['Illusie1994:1.2 arithmetic monodromy special case'],['P2-S09'],'Lie–Kolchin reduces the connected solvable image to torus-by-unipotent; a nonzero torus quotient would survive in coinvariants.',confidence='medium')
K(181,'Radical abelianization map called an isogeny','K34-01',[32],'est} une \\uline{isogénie}',
'The map R/[R,R]→H°/[H°,H°], for the solvable radicalR of an arbitrary connected linear algebraic groupH, need not be an isogeny.','false as stated','E','Counterexample H=SL2⋉Ga² with the standard action: R=Ga² and H is perfect, so the map Ga²→0 has positive-dimensional kernel. A repair may work after discarding unipotent factors, but is not the printed argument.',['MilneAV:IV2 algebraic linear representations background'],['P2-S09'],'SL2 is perfect and [SL2,Ga²]=Ga², whence H/[H,H]=0; every hypothesis is characteristic0.')
K(182,'Arithmetic local monodromy from cyclotomic infinity','K34-01',[31],'soit infinie',
'If no finite extension of residue fieldk contains all ℓ-power roots of unity, every continuous finite-dimensional Qℓ-representation of G_K for a henselian DVRK is quasi-unipotent on inertia.','proved there','A','Grothendieck’s theorem is published in Serre–Tate1968 Appendix and restated exactly in Illusie1994Theorem1.2. The condition holds for fields finitely generated over their prime field.',['SerreTate1968:Appendix','Illusie1994:1.2'],['P2-S10'])
K(183,'Potentially abelian arithmetic action has finite inertia','K34-01',[31],'essentiellement} triviale',
'Under the preceding cyclotomic-infinity condition, an essentially abelian representation of G_K has finite inertia image.','stated without proof','A','The commutator relation with the nontrivial cyclotomic action kills the unipotent inertia part in an abelian image. This strengthens quasi-unipotence only under the whole-G_K commutativity assumption.',['Illusie1994:1.2,1.5','MilneCM:6.13'],['P2-S10'],'After finite extension the image is abelian; conjugation gives N=χ(g)N for some χ(g)≠1, hence N=0.')
K(184,'Geometric local monodromy conjecture','K34-02',[34,35],'\\uline{Conjecture} 7.1.',
'For X separated of finite type over the fraction field of a henselian DVR, and ℓ invertible in the residue field, inertia acts quasi-unipotently on each H^i(X_bar,Qℓ) and H_c^i.','open on the pages','A','The theorem is established by Grothendieck; Illusie1994Theorem1.4 states the general result and explains the use of Néron smoothing for passage to arbitrary henselian DVRs. The original conjectural page is not already a proof merely because later sections settle special cases.',['Illusie1994:1.3–1.4','SerreTate1968:Appendix'],['P2-S10'])
K(185,'Degree-by-degree ℓ-independence of inertia traces','K34-02',[35],'indépendant de $\\ell\\ne p$',
'For fixed i and inertia elementg, ask whether Tr(g,H^i(X_bar,Qℓ)) is a rational integer independent ofℓ, with the same finite quotient representation after semisimplification.','open on the pages','C','For abelian varieties/H¹ the Serre–Tate/SGA7 programme provides established results. Kato2019 and Lu–Zheng AppendixA.1 prove alternating traces for general varieties, not this individual-degree assertion. The broad exact manuscript statement is therefore not labelled settled.',['Kato2019:Introduction,AppendixA.1','SerreTate1968:source family'],['P2-S11'],confidence='medium')
K(186,'Nonnegative trace requirement','K34-02',[35],'soit un entier naturel',
'Inertia trace can be a negative integer: the quadratic twist overC((t)) of a constant elliptic curve has an inertia involution acting as−1 on H¹, so trace−2.','false as stated','E','The adjective naturel is too strong; rational integer is the plausible repair. This does not refute ℓ-independence.',['Kato2019:AppendixA.1 uses rational integer'],['P2-S11'],comp='negative_inertia_trace')
K(187,'Weil-group compatibility and weights','K34-02',[35,36],'de la forme $q^{\\unsure{w|u}/2}$',
'For finite residue field, ask for rational ℓ-independent individual-degree Weil traces and integral weights0≤w≤2i for geometric Frobenius lifts; distinguish this from the weight–monodromy filtration conjecture.','open on the pages','C','Illusie1994§3 formulates the weight–monodromy problem and limits. Kato/Lu–Zheng2019AppendixA.2 proves alternating Weil-trace independence and integrality constraints. These do not settle every degreewise or common-rational-form assertion. Recent2026 searches returned specialized hyperkähler preprints but the original arXiv opening failed, so they are not used to assert a new general theorem.',['Illusie1994:§3','Kato2019:AppendixA.2'],['P2-S11'],confidence='medium')
K(188,'Common rational form of local representations','K34-02',[36],'ne s\'écrit pas en général sur $\\mathbb Q$',
'Ask whether the Γ×Ga local monodromy representations for allℓ arise from a common representation overQ; the manuscript itself warns that even the finite Γ representation can fail to descend toQ for potentially-good elliptic curves in positive residue characteristic.','corrected by himself later','C','Rational character and realizability overQ are different (Schur-index obstruction). Later Weil–Deligne compatibility over a suitable number field does not by itself give aQ-model. The margin retracts the unrestricted naive form; no specific quaternionic elliptic example is supplied here.',['Kato2019:AppendixA.1–A.2 only trace results','Illusie1994:1.5'],['P2-S11'],confidence='medium')
K(189,'Approximation of a trait by regular pairs','K34-02',[37,38,39,40],'sont \\uline{cofinaux}',
'In equal characteristic0, resolve finite-type models of a DVR together with div(t); domination by the DVR and v(t)=1 force a single normal-crossings component of multiplicity1, producing cofinal regular local pairs(A,t).','proof sketched','A','This is a conditional/local-uniformization route to arithmetic monodromy. The manuscript extends it conditionally to other characteristics. Modern general local monodromy uses a different smoothing argument (Illusie1.4); it does not prove strong resolution in positive or mixed characteristic.',['Illusie1994:1.4','ILO2014:Introduction local uniformization'],['P2-S10'],'If t=u∏x_j^{n_j} and allx_j lie in the dominating DVR maximal ideal,1=v(t)=∑n_jv(x_j); thus exactly one term and multiplicity1.')
K(190,'Normal-crossing tame fundamental group','K34-02',[42,43,44],'Démonstration laissée au lecteur',
'For a strictly henselian regular local ring and U obtained by deletingn coordinate divisors, the maximal prime-to-p quotient ofπ1(U) is(Zhat′(1))^n; over a henselian residue field the quotient carries the diagonal cyclotomic action.','stated without proof','E','This is the normal-crossings Abhyankar/Kummer theorem. The DVR case is directly verified in Stacks15.116; higher-dimensional statement requires purity/Abhyankar, not just a coordinate count. Exact scope matches regularity, henselianity and prime-to-p quotient, not full wild inertia.',['Stacks0EXT:15.116','SGA7I:§3 source family'],['P2-S10'],confidence='medium')
K(191,'Spreading proper cohomology and specialization','K34-02',[40,41,42],'chgt de base propre',
'For a proper model ofX over a finite-type approximation, constructibility permits shrinking to a lisse cohomology sheaf, and proper base change identifies the generic-fibre Galois representation; finite modℓ inertia images stabilize in a filtered system.','proof sketched','A','Proper base change and constructibility are established in SGA4; the passage from compatible finite coefficients to a single adic lisse open needs the usual adic finiteness theorem, not an arbitrary infinite intersection of opens.',['MilneLEC:17,19','ILO2014:XIII4.2.1'],['P2-S10'])
K(192,'Nonproper monodromy via generic constructibility','K34-02',[44,45],'sans hyp. de propreté',
'The spreading argument extends to nonproperX once ordinary direct-image cohomology is constructible on a common dense open and commutes with the relevant field extensions.','open on the pages','B','Deligne’s finiteness theorem, cited in Milne24.3, supplies the missing generic input in classical scope. ILOXIII gives modern excellent-scheme finiteness. This removes the manuscript’s reliance on strong resolution for this consequence, while not proving strong resolution itself.',['MilneLEC:24.3','ILO2014:XIII1.1.1,4.2.1'],['P2-S10'])
K(193,'Poincaré duality reduction between H and Hc','K34-02',[46],'Relation entre $H^i$ et $H^i_!$',
'For smooth separated pure-dimensionnX, H^i(X_bar,Qℓ)≅H_c^{2n−i}(X_bar,Qℓ)^∨(−n); inertia acts trivially on the Tate twist whenℓ≠residuechar.','proof sketched','E','Milne24.1 gives the precise duality pairing. The concluding same-i wording in the manuscript must be interpreted for all degrees, or corrected to complementary degree2n−i.',['MilneLEC:24.1','Illusie1994:1.1'],['P2-S10'])
K(194,'Localization and birational reduction','K34-02',[46,47,48],'caractère \\uline{birationnel}',
'For j:U→X and closed complementi:Y, the compact-support triangle RΓ_c(U)→RΓ_c(X)→RΓ_c(Y)→ induces the reduction: assuming monodromy in lower dimension, the property forX is equivalent to that for denseU.','proof sketched','E','Formal localization is established; the printed sequence has terms in the wrong order and an unfinished endpoint. The desired induction is correct after restoring the compact-support triangle.',['MilneLEC:18'],['P2-S10'])
K(195,'Resolution and proper descent reduction','K34-02',[48,49],'suite spectrale de descente de Deligne',
'Under resolution over the perfect closure in every dimension needed for Čech powers, quasi-unipotence for smooth projective varieties over finite purely inseparable extensions implies it for all finite-typeX, using proper cohomological descent and induction in cohomological degree.','proof sketched','A','The live revised Proposition9.6 at34:50 states the all-dimension version. The cancelled49 statement must not be revived with an insufficient fixed-dimension assumption for all Čech products. Modern theorem is unconditional (Illusie1.4).',['Illusie1994:1.4','SGA4:cohomological descent source family'],['P2-S10'])
K(196,'Finite monodromy on H0','K34-03',[50],'Le cas de $i=0$ est trivial',
'Galois acts on H⁰ of a geometric finite-type scheme through permutation of finitely many connected components, hence through a finite quotient.','stated without proof','E','Independently proved from finite component set; gives the omitted base case of the descent induction.',['MilneLEC:19 finiteness'],['P2-S10'])
K(197,'H1 reduction by normalization and descent','K34-03',[50,51],'suite spectrale de descente finie',
'For finite normalization of a reduced finite-type scheme, proper/finite descent yields0→H¹ of the cosimplicialH⁰-system→H¹(X)→ker(H¹(X′)→H¹(X′×_XX′)); the first term has finite Galois image.','proof sketched','A','Standard finite cohomological descent. The exact sequence on the page contains the neededH⁰ correction, so normalization does not identifyH¹ without a correction term.',['SGA4:cohomological descent source family','Illusie1994:1.4'],['P2-S10'])
K(198,'H1 reduction to smooth projective curves','K34-03',[51],'Proposition.} 9.8.',
'For H¹ monodromy, reduce through normal open restriction and a generic transverse curve to a smooth projective curve after a generic-parameter field extension with a suitable dominating valuation, and subsequent finite extensions as needed, using the boundary localization sequence. The required open-image condition on inertia is the separate issue C-SA13-227.','open on the pages','A','The proposition is left blank at34:51, but K34-04§5 supplies the same reduction. SGA7I3.5 and Illusie1994§1–3 establish the resulting local monodromy theorem; exact manuscript formulation remains unfinished. The Grassmannian/function-field extension is generally transcendental, not merely finite; the valuation/inertia comparison is retained as C-SA13-227.',['Illusie1994:§1–3','SGA7I:3.5 source family'],['P2-S10'])
K(199,'Finite geometric abelian coinvariants','K34-03',[53,54],'est un groupe \\uline{fini}',
'Ifk is finitely generated over its prime field, X normal geometrically connected locally of finite type, andℓ≠char k, then for every open arithmetic fundamental subgroupπ the coinvariants of its geometric pro-ℓ abelianization under the residue Galois quotient are finite.','open on the pages','E','The supplied ExposéII§§2–3 later proves this by curve reduction and finite-field specialization. Published SGA7II is MichèleRaynaud’s different finite-presentation exposé, so matching the numberII alone is wrong. Literature search did not identify an exact publication of this handwritten exposé. Its proof is substantively checkable here.',['MilneLEC:14,19','SGA7II:published title only,not exact match'],['P2-S12'],'Equivalent to H¹(X_bar,Zℓ)^{G_k}=0 after finite generation; reductions and curve proof recorded inC219–223.',confidence='medium')
K(200,'Kernel of arithmetic abelianization','K34-03',[54],'a un \\uline{noyau fini}',
'UnderC199, π^ab(ℓ)→G_k′^ab(ℓ) is surjective with finite kernel, as a quotient of the finite geometric coinvariants.','proved there','E','Formal exactness of abelianization for a group extension. The finite kernel statement concerns the pro-ℓ part on both sides.',['MilneLEC:fundamental group formalism'],['P2-S12'],'Abelianize1→I→π→π0→1 to get I_ab,π0→π_ab→π0,ab→0, then take pro-ℓ parts.')
K(201,'Virtually solvable geometric image is unipotent','K34-03',[54,55],'essentiellement unipotente',
'UnderC199, a continuousQℓ-representation ofπ1(X) whose geometric image is virtually solvable has virtually unipotent geometric image; if that restriction is semisimple, its image is finite.','proof sketched','E','Follows from the finite-coinvariant criterionC180 and characteristic-zero algebraic groups. The proof at34:55 still says abelian after the statement was enlarged to solvable; this is a revision mismatch, not a proof of the enlarged case by the words alone.',['Illusie1994:1.2 special arithmetic analogue'],['P2-S12'],confidence='medium')
K(202,'Virtually abelian total action has finite geometric image','K34-03',[55,56],'essentiellement triviale',
'UnderC199, an essentially abelian arithmetic representation becomes pulled back from a Galois representation of the constants after finite étale base change.','proved there','E','The proof uses the finite kernelC200 and an open subgroup disjoint from that kernel. This does not assert that a finite cover can be replaced by a constant-field extension.',['MilneLEC:fundamental group formalism'],['P2-S12'])
K(203,'Abelian schemes with virtually solvable geometric Tate action','K34-03',[56,57,58],'une \\uline{isogénie} \\inserted{\\uline{radicielle}}',
'For an abelian scheme over a normal geometrically integral finite-type scheme over a separably closed field, virtually solvable geometric Tate action leads after a finite étale cover to a radicial isogeny from a constant abelian scheme; in characteristic0 this is an isomorphism.','proof sketched','C','The manuscript invokes Grothendieck1966 Proposition4.4 and extends its argument. The1966 paper title/publication and citation are verified, but the exact Proposition4.4 text was not available through the opened publisher page. The primary comparison is therefore partial: geometric semisimplicity is verified, while the exact radicial constant-part descent and the scope of this adaptation still require proposition-level collation. ClassC records that specific incomplete identification; it is not a declaration that the correctly formulated modern abelian-scheme theorem is open.',['GrothHom1966:Prop4.4 manuscript citation; full text not collated','DeligneWeilII:3.4.1 geometric semisimplicity'],['P2-S13'],confidence='medium')
K(204,'Unipotent Tate action of an abelian scheme is trivial','K34-03',[58],'Alors cette action est triviale',
'In the geometric abelian-scheme situation ofC203, unipotent Tate action is trivial and the scheme is radicially isogenous to a constant one.','stated without proof','C','The page calls this an adaptation of Grothendieck1966Prop4.4 using Mordell–Weil/trace descent. Verified geometric semisimplicity removes nontrivial unipotent geometric monodromy, but the constant/radicial descent additionally needs the abelian-scheme theorem whose exact Proposition4.4 text was not collated. ClassC marks this precise source-identification gap, not a newly open modern conjecture.',['GrothHom1966:Prop4.4 cited,not collated','DeligneWeilII:3.4.1'],['P2-S13'],confidence='medium')
K(205,'Full level structure kills finite twisting','K34-03',[58,59],'entier $n\\geq3$',
'In characteristic0, a finite isotrivial abelian scheme with trivial fulln-torsion for n≥3 has trivial twisting, hence is constant. Finite-order automorphisms congruent to1 modulo n≥3 are identity.','proof sketched','E','Classical level-structure rigidity. The printed ambiguous abélienne|unipotente affects which preceding theorem supplies isotriviality; both the finite-image conclusion and the finite-order hypothesis matter.',['MilneAV:I15–16 moduli/families'],['P2-S13'],'A nontrivial unipotent infinite-order automorphism can be1 modn, so the finite twisting group cannot be dropped.')
K(206,'Constant extension prescribed in arithmetic isotriviality','K34-03',[59,60],'$K\'=Kk\'$',
'An essentially solvable Tate representation can imply potential isotriviality, but the required finite extension ofK need not be a compositum with a finite extension of its prime field.','false as stated','B','The proof on34:60 uses a finite étale coverX′, which is more general thanK′=Kk′. The CM quadratic twisty²=x³−t²x overQ(t) becomes constant overQ(t,√t), while no finite constant extension removes its geometric inertia. Thus the literal constant-extension formulation is false; the finite-extension correction is the plausible theorem.',['GrothHom1966:constant-part source family','MilneCM:CM Tate action'],['P2-S13'],comp='nonconstant_extension_needed')
K(207,'Potential good reduction of potentially abelian abelian varieties','K34-03',[61,62,65,66],'Bonne réduction',
'For an abelian variety over a number field with potentially abelian Tate representation, potential good reduction holds at all finite places; a single finite extension can give good reduction everywhere.','proof sketched','B','Theℓ≠pplaces follow from arithmetic monodromy and Néron–Ogg–Shafarevich. Thep-place argument requires a different theorem; Serre–Tate1968 and modern CM theory establish the conclusion. The unfinished phrase at34:61 is not itself a completed proof.',['SerreTate1968:Theorems1–2','MilneCM:6.13–6.14'],['P2-S13'])
K(208,'Potentially solvable Tate image implies CM','K34-03',[62,63],'soit de type CM',
'For an abelian variety over a number field, virtually solvableℓ-adic Galois image implies CM after finite extension. The same geometric property over a characteristic-zero finitely generated field requires reduction to the constant part.','open on the pages','B','Established via Faltings semisimplicity and endomorphism theorem: the connected solvable reductive image is a torus, and the commutant criterion gives CM. The manuscript’s concern about semisimplicity was substantive at the time. Faltings’s number-field theorems were published1983; no first-circulation date is claimed.',['MilneAV:IV2.5,2.6,2.7','MilneCM:3.3'],['P2-S13'],'After finite extension, semisimplicity makes the connected envelope reductive; solvable+reductive=torus. Its action has commutative image algebra, and the Tate endomorphism equality/double centralizer yields a maximal étale subalgebra ofEnd⁰ of degree2g.')
K(209,'Extension of the solvable-monodromy argument to motives','K34-03',[63],'une fois la théorie des motifs assise',
'Seek the analogue of the abelian-scheme conclusions for geometric cohomology/motives of arbitrary weight, contingent on suitable motivic foundations, semisimplicity and realization comparison.','open on the pages','C','Geometric semisimplicity for smooth proper direct images is established; arithmetic semisimplicity of arbitrary motivicGalois representations is a stronger general problem. The manuscript does not formulate a single unconditional motivic theorem.',['DeligneWeilII:3.4.1','MilneCM:4.7 and motives remarks'],['P2-S14'],confidence='medium')
K(210,'Geometric semisimplicity conjecture','K34-03',[56,59,63,64],'on conjecture',
'For f smooth proper over a normal connected base over an algebraically closed field andℓ invertible, the lisseQℓ sheafR^if_*Qℓ is geometrically semisimple. This is distinct from arithmetic Frobenius semisimplicity.','open on the pages','B','Deligne’s geometric semisimplicity theorem is WeilII3.4.1 (1980; received1979), with characteristic-zero Hodge-theoretic antecedents. The cancelled abelian-scheme footnote is replaced by a direct argument; the later explicit smooth-f footnote remains a genuine conjecture on these pages.',['DeligneWeilII:3.4.1'],['P2-S14'])
K(211,'Finite-field Frobenius is semisimple on abelian Tate modules','K34-03',[65],'l\'action du frobénius',
'For an abelian variety over a finite field, geometric/arithmetic Frobenius acts semisimply onVℓ forℓ≠p.','proved there','E','Frobenius is central inEnd⁰ over the finite base, whose centre is a product of number fields; its minimal polynomial is squarefree. MilneAVII2.9 and discussion verify the standard theorem. The inverse convention does not affect semisimplicity.',['MilneAV:II2.9 andfollowing discussion'],['P2-S13'])
K(212,'Nilpotent global image becomes a torus','K34-03',[65,66],'$U=(1)$, cqfd.',
'For the Tate representation of an abelian scheme over a normal arithmetic finite-type base, if the connected algebraic image is nilpotent then it is a torus: semisimple Frobenius elements are dense and lie in the torus factor.','proved there','E','The marked obligation A prouverU=1 is discharged on the same page. The argument uses Chebotarev density and the fact that a connected nilpotent linear algebraic group in characteristic0 is torus×unipotent. It is not a proof for arbitrary solvable groups.',['MilneAV:II2.9 finite-field semisimplicity','DeligneSerre1974:3.2 density/characters'],['P2-S13'])
K(213,'Potential good reduction from inertia semisimplicity,ℓ≠p','K34-04',[67,68],'bonne réduction potentielle',
'For an abelian variety over a henselian discretely valued field andℓ distinct from residue characteristic, potential good reduction is equivalent to finite inertia image and equivalent to semisimplicity of the inertia representation.','proof sketched','B','Serre–Tate1968Theorem2 gives potential good reduction iff finite inertia image; Grothendieck quasi-unipotence makes semisimple inertia image finite, and finite-group characteristic-zero representations are semisimple.',['SerreTate1968:Theorem2','Illusie1994:1.2–1.4'],['P2-S13'])
K(214,'Potential good reduction does not forcep-adic inertia semisimplicity','K34-04',[67],'et alors seulement',
'Whenℓ equals residue characteristicp, potential good reduction does not imply thatV_p(A)|I is semisimple. An ordinary good-reduction elliptic lift with non-torsion Serre–Tate extension parameter gives a nonsplit extension on inertia.','false as stated','B','Katz’s Serre–Tate local moduli§2 produces good-reduction deformations with nontrivial extension parameter. Brinon–Conrad2009§8.3 analyzes nonsplit crystalline extensions; finite-flat/crystalline good reduction is not semisimplicity. Thus the parenthetical converse needs deletion/restriction, pending ink inspection.',['Katz1981:§2','BrinonConrad2009:8.3.6–8.3.7'],['P2-S13'],'Choose a non-torsion ordinary Serre–Tate parameter, so the rational connected–étale Tate extension is nonsplit. Its rank-one coefficient restricts toQp(1) on inertia and has no inertia invariants. Inflation–restriction injects its globalH¹ into inertiaH¹, preserving nonsplitting. All fibres have good reduction; the counterexample addresses only the converse.',confidence='medium')
K(215,'Semisimplep-adic inertia implies potential good reduction','K34-04',[67,68],'le cas de la car résiduelle',
'For an abelian variety over a mixed-characteristic henselian trait, a semisimpleV_p inertia representation should force potential good reduction by semistable reduction and the toric quotient argument.','proof sketched','C','The manuscript gives only the sufficiency argument after referring to a future exposé. Original SGA7IX5.13 is a Barsotti–Tate good-reduction criterion, not the literal semisimplicity equivalence. Exact general scope of this sufficiency claim was not collated; theℓ≠pversion is secure and thepconverse is false.',['BrinonConrad2009:7.1.13','SGA7IX:5.12–5.13,requiresBTdefinitions'],['P2-S13'],confidence='medium')
K(216,'Cross-ℓ rational abelian representations proposal','K34-04',[68,69],'loc} rationnelle',
'Compare locally algebraic abelian representations at differentℓ with common rational Frobenius polynomials; ask for control of the other image and semisimplification, as a route toCM.','open on the pages','C','Serre’s locally algebraic character theory and later Faltings settle theCM consequence, but the handwritten sentence is unfinished after choosingℓ. Equality of Frobenius polynomials determines a semisimple representation over a fixed coefficient field; it does not identify arbitrary extensions.',['DeligneSerre1974:3.2','MilneAV:IV2.5–2.7'],['P2-S13'],confidence='medium')
K(217,'Dense open induces surjection onπ1','K34-03',[51,57],'est surjectif',
'For a normal integral schemeX and nonempty openU, π1(U)→π1(X) is surjective because connected finite étale covers ofX stay connected onU.','stated without proof','E','Standard étale fundamental-group criterion. Normality/geometric normality matters in the geometric version; it cannot be deduced merely from the normality of an arbitrary nonperfect base change.',['MilneLEC:3','SGA4VII:étale-site comparison'],['P2-S12'],'A connected finite étale cover of a normal integral scheme is normal integral; removing the inverse image of a proper closed subset preserves connectedness.')
K(218,'Purely inseparable extension and normalization','K34-04',[70,71],'induit un \\uline{isom}',
'Universal homeomorphisms, including finite purely inseparable field extension and finite radicial normalization in the geometrically unibranch situation, induce equivalences of étale sites and isomorphisms ofπ1.','proof sketched','E','Standard topological invariance of the étale site, within the stated universal-homeomorphism hypotheses. The page’sX/X′ labels need checking; normalization is not universally radicial for every scheme.',['SGA4VII:topological invariance framework','MilneLEC:étale-site invariance'],['P2-S12'])
K(219,'Coinvariants reformulated asH1 invariants','K34-04',[72],'$H_{\\deleted{\\unsure{1}}}^1',
'For finitely generatedZℓ moduleV=π1(X_bar)^ab(ℓ), V_{G_k} is finite iff(Hom(V,Zℓ))^{G_k}=0, equivalentlyH¹(X_bar,Zℓ)^{G_k}=0.','proved there','E','Elementary finite-generation and duality. This uses dual invariants, notV^{G_k} itself. The converse finite criterion fails without finite generation.',['MilneLEC:19'],['P2-S12'],'A finitely generatedZℓ module has zero tensorQℓ iff it is finite. Dual of the rational coinvariants is the invariant dual.')
K(220,'Boundary sequence for an open curve and twist','K34-04',[73,74],'l\'hom somme',
'ForU=C\D, C smooth proper geometrically connected, D consists ofnrational points,0→H¹(C_bar,Zℓ)→H¹(U_bar,Zℓ)→Zℓ(−1)^n→Zℓ(−1)→0, with last map sum. Thus the quotient isZℓ(−1)^{n−1}.','proved there','E','Purity/Gysin gives the exact sequence; Milne16.1,16.7. The last paragraph of34:73 changes−1to+1 and the support should be on the compact curveC, notUwhere the deleted points are absent. These are mathematical reading alarms.',['MilneLEC:16.1,16.7'],['P2-S12'])
K(221,'NoFrobenius-fixedH1 for proper curves over finite fields','K34-04',[74],'car $A(k)$ fini',
'For a smooth projective geometrically connected curve over a finite field, H¹(C_bar,Zℓ)^{G_k}=0. This follows from finiteness ofJac(C)(k), without invoking the full Riemann hypothesis.','proved there','E','The page explicitly deletes the Weil-weight argument in favour of finiteness of rational points. ForF−1 on a finite-rank Tate module, zero rational kernel implies finite cokernel; duality gives the assertedH¹ invariants.',['MilneAV:II1–2','MilneLEC:14'],['P2-S12'],'A compatibleℓ-power torsion sequence fixed byF lies in the finite groupA(k), hence iszero. ConsequentlyF−1 is invertible onVℓA.')
K(222,'Invariants and coinvariants differ for generalGalois groups','K34-04',[74],'ne suffit pas',
'Vanishing ofTℓ(A)^{G_K} does not alone imply finiteTℓ(A)_{G_K}; the latter is dual to invariants in the dual representation.','stated without proof','E','The manuscript warning is mathematically sound; a two-generator linear-algebra example demonstrates the logical gap. The specificK=Qℓ example is a lead whose geometric realization is not supplied on the page.',['MilneAV:IV2 representation background'],['P2-S12'],comp='invariants_vs_coinvariants')
K(223,'Generic curve surjects on fundamental groups','K34-04',[75,76,77,78],'sont \\uline{surjectifs}',
'For a geometrically normal connected projectiveX′ and dense openX, the generic complete-intersection curveC over the Grassmannian function field gives a surjectionπ1(C_bar)→π1(X_bar), and compatible surjections of arithmetic extensions.','proof sketched','A','Bertini connectedness for pullbacks of connected finite étale covers is the actual argument. The generic parameterfield is finitely generated, preserving the field hypothesis inC199. The basepoint-image assertiondimclosure{x}≥1 may need refinement and is not used to strengthen the theorem.',['SGA1:generic hyperplane/fundamental group source family','MilneLEC:3,31'],['P2-S12'],confidence='medium')
K(224,'Prime-to-p fundamental group is finitely generated','K34-04',[78],'topologique à engendrement fini',
'For a connected finite-type scheme over a separably closed field of characteristicp, its maximal prime-to-p quotient ofπ1 is topologically finitely generated.','proof sketched','A','Classical SGA1/SGA7 finiteness; published SGA7II discusses stronger finite presentation for surfaces and conditionally in higher dimension. Do not substitute finite presentation for the manuscript finite-generation assertion.',['SGA7II:Introduction','MilneLEC:3'],['P2-S12'])
K(225,'Almost allℓ geometric coinvariants vanish','K34-04',[79,80],'Pour presque tout $\\ell$',
'InC199, for all but finitely manyℓ, geometric pro-ℓ abelian coinvariants vanish, equivalentlyH¹(X_bar,Fℓ)^{G_k}=0.','proved there','E','The supplied proof reduces to a curve and a fixed finite-field model, excluding primes dividing the roots-of-unity group and the finite Jacobian rational-point group. This is stronger than finite coinvariants for eachℓ. Exact independent publication of the handwritten exposé was not identified.',['MilneAV:II1–2','MilneLEC:16'],['P2-S12'],'For a finite endomorphismF−1 ofA[ℓ], kernel and cokernel have equal cardinality; ifℓ∤#A(k), both vanish.',comp='finite_kernel_cokernel')
K(226,'Finite-field abelianπ1 target typo','K34-04',[80],'$\\pi_1(\\mathbb F_p)\\simeq\\widehat{\\mathbb Z}$',
'For normal geometrically connectedX/F_q, π1(X)^ab(ℓ)→G_Fq^ab(ℓ)=Zℓ has finite kernel, and is an isomorphism for almost allℓ.','false as stated','E','The literal pro-ℓ source cannot surject onto fullZhat. The field must beF_q or a correctly normalized inclusion intoG_Fp, and the target must be its pro-ℓ part. The repaired assertion follows fromC200/C225.',['MilneLEC:3'],['P2-S12'])
K(227,'Monodromy reduction via a dominating valuation','K34-04',[81],'est d\'indice fini',
'To transferH¹ monodromy from the generic curve overK(t) back toK, choose a prolongation of the valuation with suitable residue/ramification control; the induced inertia image must be open.','stated without proof','C','The note says an arbitrary dominating valuation works, but the necessary discreteness and residue-extension hypotheses are not given. Classical monodromy proofs choose controlled valuations. The exact unrestricted assertion is not accepted solely from this note.',['Illusie1994:1.4 andgeometric proof','Stacks09EE:inertia for finiteDVRextensions only'],['P2-S10'],confidence='medium')
K(228,'H1 nilpotence echelon','K34-04',[81,82],'unipotente d\'\\uline{échelon} 2',
'For a smooth separated curve over a henselian discretely valued field,ℓ≠residuechar, after finite extension inertia acts onH¹ withN²=0. The manuscript reductions give echelon≤2fornormalX and≤3forgeneralX through a finite-descent extension.','open on the pages','A','Published SGA7I3.5 states theH¹ level2 result for arbitrary finite-typeX, stronger than the draft general bound3. This exact locator is a source-family match pending original-page collation; Illusie1994 describes geometric monodromy and its nilpotence bounds. The34:81 concludingH² must beH¹ for the announced reduction.',['SGA7I:3.5 source-family match','Illusie1994:§1–3'],['P2-S10'],confidence='medium')
K(229,'Radical of geometric cohomological monodromy','K34-03',[63,64],'radical $R$ de $G^\\circ$ est unipotent',
'For a lisse geometric cohomology sheaf on a normal connected geometric base, the connected algebraic monodromy group has unipotent radical in the solvable-radical sense once the finite-coinvariant argument applies. If the sheaf is also semisimple, its connected group is semisimple.',
'proof sketched','B','Deligne’s geometric semisimplicity/purity framework supplies established smooth-proper cases. The statement must useπ1(Y), notπ1(X), and locally constant tordu rather than literally constantR^if_*. Unconditional arbitrary nonproper cases do not follow from the incomplete page.',['DeligneWeilII:3.4.1','ILO2014:XIII finiteness'],['P2-S14'],confidence='medium')
K(230,'Cancelled nonabelian solvable pro-ℓ extension','K34-03',[64],'il est plausible',
'The cancelled remark proposes a nonabelian analogue of finite coinvariants for aπ-stable solvable pro-ℓ quotientHof geometricπ1, with a commutator subgroup said to be open in an ambiguous group.',
'open on the pages','F','The remark is wholly cancelled and the subgroup[π0,H] is not intrinsically defined without choosing an action/extension; openness inπ′ versusH changes the assertion. Weight theory is relevant but does not identify this exact discarded conjecture.',['DeligneWeilII:weights/semisimplicity framework'],['P2-S14'],confidence='low')

def save():
 for c in claims:
  c['statement']=c['modern_statement'];c['status_on_pages']=c['page_status'];c['class']=c['classification'];c['fate']=c['literature_fate'];c['refs']=list(dict.fromkeys(x.split(':')[0] for x in c['references']));c['computation']=c['computations']
 (OUT/'claims.json').write_text(json.dumps(claims,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n')
 print(f'Saved {len(claims)} manually authored claim records; IDs {claims[0]["id"]}..{claims[-1]["id"]}.')
if __name__=='__main__': save()
